<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Oboi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1610">1610</a>–<a href="/years/1669">1669</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Aobai</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Oboi was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchu</a> military commander and courtier of the Gūwalgiya clan who served three emperors of the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing dynasty</a>. Registered under the Bordered Yellow Banner, he won a hereditary captaincy and the honorific Baturu, brave warrior, in the wars against the Ming, and fought in the Qing conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> from 1644. In 1661 the dying <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shunzhi-emperor">Shunzhi Emperor</a> named him one of four regents for the young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kangxi-emperor">Kangxi Emperor</a>; Oboi reversed Shunzhi's policies and pushed to reassert Manchu power over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-chinese">Han Chinese</a>. In 1669 the Kangxi Emperor had him arrested on 30 charges and imprisoned, and he died that year; he was later rehabilitated, with a pardon in 1713.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oboi.jpg" alt="Oboi" width="330" height="513" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anonymous Qing court painter · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Oboi.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
